    "segments": [
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 6,
        "sourceEnd": 361,
        "text": "Okay, everyone, thanks for coming. This is the Riverbend town council's informal session on the proposed plastic bag fee. I'm Maya, the council's policy analyst. Let's start with the town level. If we introduce a five-cent fee per bag, we expect shops to reduce bag use by about thirty percent in the first year. That's a conditional claim, not a promise.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "ad02aca7def0d2544961b7643aa31bbc7869311f5c951d5c7279af556278237e",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "daniel",
        "speaker": "Daniel",
        "sourceStart": 371,
        "sourceEnd": 566,
        "text": "Sorry, Maya, can I push back on that? Thirty percent sounds high. In other towns, the drop was closer to fifteen percent. So unless we add a public information campaign, I doubt we'll hit thirty.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "ee326f062a33474bfbf0d5a6f70fe69bc7fa1783447b7d6579dc472591038530",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 574,
        "sourceEnd": 736,
        "text": "Fair point, Daniel. Let me correct myself. The thirty percent figure assumes a campaign. Without one, fifteen percent is more realistic. So the condition matters.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "fd5c24c76f1960f726484cfd3949652a9d3f0ca7fa71c7feee112ec50ab6f7e7",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "priya",
        "speaker": "Priya",
        "sourceStart": 745,
        "sourceEnd": 1013,
        "text": "I'm Priya from the Riverbend Retail Association. Even if the fee is small, our members worry about paperwork. Although the fee is only five cents, the reporting form is two pages long. That's a burden for small shops, especially those with limited staff and resources.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "f92da87bda850e395738ad35e215d1b2d0b6404220e7c0d038a6022a1a59d1ec",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1021,
        "sourceEnd": 1279,
        "text": "That's a valid concern. We could simplify the form. If the form is one page, compliance should improve. And because small shops have fewer staff, a simple form helps them most. We should also consider offering online submission to reduce administrative work.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "77ed3f3fafb964373d1d0276e841615029c9d2d301d676677b927b9b2b9dbcc8",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "daniel",
        "speaker": "Daniel",
        "sourceStart": 1289,
        "sourceEnd": 1518,
        "text": "But what about the regional level? The county wants a uniform rule. If Riverbend acts alone, shoppers may drive to the next town to avoid the fee. That's leakage. And leakage means we lose both revenue and environmental benefits.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "bfbb74cddac3b73b46fae964b0f30f42ba8035c0b8d1e43448548abd73e65fd3",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "priya",
        "speaker": "Priya",
        "sourceStart": 1527,
        "sourceEnd": 1774,
        "text": "Exactly. So even though a town fee is popular, a county-wide rule would be more effective. Although it's harder to pass, it closes the leakage gap. Our members would rather have one consistent rule across the county than a patchwork of local fees.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "6ae5dbe5746bfbe974aadff11adbb53c29e1e7dbf01ba0518e968a8334658958",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1782,
        "sourceEnd": 1973,
        "text": "Right. So the town fee works only if neighboring towns cooperate. Otherwise, the environmental gain is smaller than we hope. We should coordinate with county officials to align our timelines.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "06251cf4538da0140575f4bd4d3db2678a400ac4143834926f17610f23efe441",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "daniel",
        "speaker": "Daniel",
        "sourceStart": 1983,
        "sourceEnd": 2202,
        "text": "And the state level? The state is considering a ban on thin plastic bags altogether. If the state bans them, the town fee becomes irrelevant for those bags. But a ban would also require enforcement and public education.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "0409a0270ebb8913ca34daa4c13a9a95acb264f8b5f37983976cb30ba695deca",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 2210,
        "sourceEnd": 2426,
        "text": "True. But a state ban takes two years. So in the short term, the town fee still matters. Because the state timeline is slow, local action fills the gap. We can also use the town fee as a pilot to inform state policy.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "e286b572291fd0e5739d803134ba249370250a8cd541b64d0708cfc3e3be9e1a",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "priya",
        "speaker": "Priya",
        "sourceStart": 2435,
        "sourceEnd": 2663,
        "text": "Our members would accept a town fee if the state ban is coming. They prefer one clear rule to three confusing ones. Although they dislike fees, they dislike uncertainty more. A clear timeline from the state would help them plan.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "e8ed7f7ec81dab3b53fb0d249951595fdac94cd5186787c3d8f3aa13ff7b12d6",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 2671,
        "sourceEnd": 3067,
        "text": "Good. So here's the summary. The town fee reduces bags only if we run a campaign and simplify the form. The county rule is stronger because it prevents leakage. The state ban is the cleanest, but it is slow. Since each scale has a different speed, we should act at the town level now and support the county and state plans. This layered approach ensures we make progress while avoiding confusion.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "ee8fa60c9b592e272ba06999bb7be4638db5df51c927bf0eccec72ab9fa1dc40",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "daniel",
        "speaker": "Daniel",
        "sourceStart": 3077,
        "sourceEnd": 3240,
        "text": "One more correction. Earlier I said fifteen percent without a campaign. Actually, the study said twelve percent. So twelve, not fifteen. I apologize for the error.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "8e587d135b847809e06d12e6c905233ef8b19fba1099a52cb8d642d799d4a9d1",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 3248,
        "sourceEnd": 3406,
        "text": "Thanks, Daniel. Twelve percent without a campaign, thirty with one. Let's note that. Any final questions? No? Then we'll draft the proposal. Thanks, everyone.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "5f4fd91ccb5152730bb0957fdf9ca1731b5536fd440a0ddba49807512e4df38e",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      }
    ]
  },
